 TieredPercentage = 1.3m},
                new ProjectMapModelView { ClassOfCost = ClassOfCost.Recurring.ToDescription(), WbsNumber = "4.01.05.04", CostCenter = "xxyy", ActivityID = "BSW1016", ActivityName = "COFT Support", WbsElementTitle = "Chief of Flight Test Support", InitialResource = "4143BWP", StartDate = DateTime.Parse("06/01/2018"), EndDate = DateTime.Parse("06/30/2019"), Clin = "0001", SowNumber = "MOT", SowTitle = "PROJECT PLANNING AND MANAGEMENT, PRODUCT TEST, EVALUATION AND QUALIFICATION", Task = "LOE for Test Supervision / Task Management, Chief of Flight Test (4143BWP):\r\nWPB DFC Chief of Flight Test (COFT) is to actively manage the Development Flight Center's (DFC) flight test program planning, financial performance, and daily statusing of the SANG program.  The COFT is responsible for the support of all flight test related activities.  The scope of responsibility includes both technical direction and financial management.  The COFT provides daily consistent coordination and communication between the Development Flight Center (DFC) and the Programs Chief of Test (COT / 4450AF8 resource).  The COFT publishes all DFC daily and weekly status reports and coordinates all DFC inputs related to scope.  In conjunction with the COT, the COFT is also responsible of budget and schedule on all flight test activities, developing internal project plan / schedule, interfacing with DCMA and GFR, and ensuring the availability of DFC resources for the contracted program.  While the COT is the responsible EVMS CAM (Control Account Manager) for all Development Test activities, the COFT is the WPM (Work Package Manager) for the flight test activities.", Hours = 800, Dollars = 0, Rationale = "4143BWP (Chief of Flight Test) hours are a function of the total non-recurring Development Flight Center (DFC) T&E Salary and Hourly (Hangar Crew) personnel hours. This due to the fact that the COFT interfaces and coordinates with individuals in these organizations during the course of a test program.  Salary hours include all W, X, and Y branch personnel, P3 DFC site personnel, and 4049B Safety.  Hourly (Hangar Crew) hours include any DFC Hourly personnel, including Hourly QA.  Hourly personnel from the Florida Assembly and Flight Operations (FAFO) center are included as well, for their efforts in direct support of the Development Test  At times, it is more efficient to complete an element of the Development Test at the manufacturing facility.  During these times, Manufacuting Hourly are utilized in place of the DFC Hourly and are therefore included. \r\n\r\nNote that 4045FF1 (Stratford, not DFC), and 4450AF8 (Stratford, not DFC) are not included is this evaluation.\r\n\r\nFor this S70I effort, the level of effort required by the COFT will be siimilar to that utilized during the recent SANG AS IOC Develoment Test effort completed in 2014.  Development Flight Test activities for SANG AS IOC were completed under WBS EV-BK3.4.01.01, EV-BK3.4.01.02, and EV-BK3.4.01.05.  Within these WBS elements, total 4143BWP hours were 289.87.  Total other non-recurring Development Flight Center (DFC) T&E Salary and Hourly (Hangar Crew) personnel hours were 2489.91.  Therefore, the COFT hours were 11.6% of that total.\r\n\r\nTotal estimated non-recurring Development Flight Center (DFC) T&E Salary and Hourly (Hangar Crew) hours for this temperature and vibration survey effort are 6,357.  \r\n6,357 hours x 0.116 = 737 hours for the COFT. \r\n\r\n", CamName = "Stange, B. ", Category = "ENG", TieredPercentage = 1.3m},
                new ProjectMapModelView { ClassOfCost = ClassOfCost.Recurring.ToDescription(), WbsNumber = "4.01.05.04", CostCenter = "xxyy", ActivityID = "BSW1017", ActivityName = "COT Support", WbsElementTitle = "Chief of Test Support", InitialResource = "4045FF8", StartDate = DateTime.Parse("02/06/2018"), EndDate = DateTime.Parse("06/30/2019"), Clin = "0001", SowNumber = "MOT", SowTitle = "PROJECT PLANNING AND MANAGEMENT, PRODUCT TEST, EVALUATION AND QUALIFICATION", Task = "LOE for Test Supervision / Task Management, Chief of Test (4450AF8):\r\nSMS Army Engineering Chief of Test (COT) is to actively manage Test and Evaluation resources, financial performance, and schedule performance during the planning, conduct, and reporting phases of the SANG program.  The COT is responsible for the planning and execution of all test activities (ground and flight) associated with the certification of an aircraft.  The scope of responsibility includes both technical direction and financial management.  The COT serves as an interface between Test, Design, Integration Product Teams, Program Management, and the customer (U.S. Army and/or SANG).  Responsibilities include but are not limited to:  test program planning and administration, developing internal project plan/schedule, and developing EVMS test plan.  The Chief of Test’s CAM (Control Account Manager) responsibilities include:  EV monthly status, test CDRL management, developing corrective action plans to resolve undesirable trends, and producing weekly reports to program and functional management.  ", Hours = 990, Dollars = 0, Rationale = "The Test and Evaluation (T&E) COT hours are a function of the total non-recurring T&E Salary and Development Flight Center (DFC) Hourly (Hangar Crew) personnel hours.  The hours for projects having less than 20,000 (20K) total NRE T&E hours is 14.5% with a Y-intercept of 81 COT hours; this was based on the linear regression formula of Y = 0.1452x + 81.394. Total T&E hours (under the DT&E WBS .4.01) in this task are 6269 hours for T&E salary and DFC hourly.  (0.145 * 6269 h) + 81 h = 990 hours for the SMS Army Engineering Chief of Test.\r\n\r\n\r\nNote that 4049B (Product Safety, not T&E) and 4043PP3 Pilots are not included in this Total T&E hours evaluation.\r\n\r\n", CamName = "Stange, B. ", Category = "ENG", TieredPercentage = 1.3m},
                new ProjectMapModelView { ClassOfCost = ClassOfCost.Recurring.ToDescription(), WbsNumber = "4.01.05.04", CostCenter = "xxyy", ActivityID = "BSW1015", ActivityName = "Provide Test Support of Avionics MOT Development", WbsElementTitle = "Test Director Support", InitialResource = "4043WWI", StartDate = DateTime.Parse("06/01/2018"), EndDate = DateTime.Parse("01/15/2019"), Clin = "0001", SowNumber = "MOT", SowTitle = "PROJECT PLANNING AND MANAGEMENT, PRODUCT TEST, EVALUATION AND QUALIFICATION", Task = "2014 Test Aircraft #1, Test Director:\r\nTest Director (4043WWI) is to lead the test requirements for Aircraft #1 and manage Test Aircraft usage.  The Aircraft Test Director (ATD) is responsible for overall ownership of a specific aircraft during test activities. This includes overall oversight of the technical activities to provide for efficient / effective test execution and for management of the flight risks during test execution. The ATD is the primary point of contact when their assigned A/C is in flight status. The ATD must work closely with the Chief of Flight Test (4143BWP) for program direction to ensure tasks are aligned with program objectives. The ATD in turn must also work closely with the assigned Aircraft Manager (4143FXA) to ensure their assigned aircraft meets the intended flight rates while remaining in full compliance with maintenance and inspection requirements.", Hours = 340, Dollars = 0, Rationale = "MOT testing will take 8.6 weeks (2 months) to complete, plus 1 month (4.2 weeks) planning = 12.8 weeks duration for LOE.  One month of planning is to coordinate any Test Plans required with Hangar Management and the Aircraft Manager for Temp/Vibe Survey instrumented testing, and Avionics/Functional Flight Test.\r\n\r\nAircraft Test Director (4043WWI) LOE man-hours = 0.80 WI x 40 hours/week x number of weeks = 0.8 WI x 40h/wk x 12.8 wks = 409.6 man-hours.\r\n\r\nGeneral Program Support “Level of Effort” (LOE) for a WPB Aircraft Test Director is 0.80 heads for the duration of Development Test for each test aircraft.  Specific duration for each aircraft is from a date one month prior to start of Aircraft Receiving Inspection at the Development Flight Center (DFC induction) to the date of Contractor Flight Acceptance (CFA) for that aircraft, or when aircraft is transitioned back to FAFO for customer delivery preparation.  While the test aircraft is under DFC control, the Test Director is responsible for its daily activities scheduled.\r\n\r\nActuals from the S-70M-02 Direct Commercial Sale (DCS) program and the S-70i DCS program were used.  S-70M-02 DCS charge numbers for Test Director Clock # S35742: EV-BER.3.01.01.01.00.004 (439.5 hrs), EV-BER.3.01.03.01.00.003 (3 hrs), and EV-BER.3.01.03.01.00.005 (3 hrs) were analyzed over the date range May 16, 2012 to August 31, 2012.  S-70i DCS charge numbers for Test Director Clock # C28239:  EV-BEC.3.01.01.03.01.003 (226.5 hrs), EV-BEC.3.01.02.01.02.001 (107 hrs), EV-BEC.3.01.02.02.02.005 (87 hrs), and EV-BEC.3.01.05.02.00.001 (2 hrs) were analyzed over the date range June 4, 2012 to September 4, 2012.\r\n\r\nActual man-hours for Test Director were 445.5 hours (Clock # S35742) for DCS S-70M over a 14.6 week period and 422.5 hours (Clock # C28239) for DCS S-70i over a 12.6 week period.  Using the 2012 SAC calendar, work days for the months of May to Aug 2012 were calculated (May = 168 hours, June = 168 hours, July = 152 hours, and August = 184 hours) and used to calculate equivalent Aircraft Test Director heads.\r\n\r\nFor DCS S-70M, Test Director (Clock # S35742) charged 445.5 hours during a period where 588 hours were available to work.  445.5h / 588h = 0.76 equivalent heads for the duration.\r\nFor DCS S-70i, Test Director (Clock # C28239) charged 422.5 hours during a period where 504 hours were available to work.  422.5h / 504h = 0.84 equivalent heads for the duration.\r\n\r\nThe assumption for S-70M SANG, which compares well to the DCS S-70M and DCS S-70i efforts, is that 0.8 heads of Aircraft Test Director (the average of DCS S-70M and DCS S-70i, (0.76+0.84) / 2 = 0.8 heads) will be required.\r\n\r\nRemoved 4.3 weeks due to reduction from 22 to 10 flight hours.  Also, brought WI up to full time from 0.8 time due to test at Stewart and no multi-task with other DFC projects.\r\n1.0 WI x 40 hrs/wk x 8.5 weeks = 340 hours\r\n ", CamName = "Stange, B. ", Category = "ENG", TieredPercentage = 1.3m},
                new ProjectMapModelView { ClassOfCost = ClassOfCost.Recurring.ToDescription(), WbsNumber = "4.01.05.04", CostCenter = "xxyy", ActivityID = "BSW1042", ActivityName = "Provide Test Support of Avionics MOT Development", WbsElementTitle = "Test Director Support", InitialResource = "4043WWI", StartDate = DateTime.Parse("06/01/2018"), EndDate = DateTime.Parse("01/15/2019"), Clin = "0001", SowNumber = "MOT", SowTitle = "PROJECT PLANNING AND MANAGEMENT, PRODUCT TEST, EVALUATION AND QUALIFICATION", Task = "2014 Test Aircraft #1, Test Director:\r\nTest Director (4043WWI) is to lead the test requirements for Aircraft #1 and manage Test Aircraft usage.  The Aircraft Test Director (ATD) is responsible for overall ownership of a specific aircraft during test activities. This includes overall oversight of the technical activities to provide for efficient / effective test execution and for management of the flight risks during test execution. The ATD is the primary point of contact when their assigned A/C is in flight status. The ATD must work closely with the Chief of Flight Test (4143BWP) for program direction to ensure tasks are aligned with program objectives. The ATD in turn must also work closely with the assigned Aircraft Manager (4143FXA) to ensure their assigned aircraft meets the intended flight rates while remaining in full compliance with maintenance and inspection requirements.", Hours = 340, Dollars = 0, Rationale = "MOT testing will take 8.6 weeks (2 months) to complete, plus 1 month (4.2 weeks) planning = 12.8 weeks duration for LOE.  One month of planning is to coordinate any Test Plans required with Hangar Management and the Aircraft Manager for Temp/Vibe Survey instrumented testing, and Avionics/Functional Flight Test.\r\n\r\nAircraft Test Director (4043WWI) LOE man-hours = 0.80 WI x 40 hours/week x number of weeks = 0.8 WI x 40h/wk x 12.8 wks = 409.6 man-hours.\r\n\r\nGeneral Program Support “Level of Effort” (LOE) for a WPB Aircraft Test Directo